# Debian stable: a machine where `apt` is already the right answer and the installer refuses it
# anyway.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE IS THAT DEBIAN NOW WORKS, and it used to be the opposite. Debian 13
# (trixie, current stable) is an apt distro, so install_podman's `apt-get` (installer:591) was
# always correct here -- what turned it away was the version floor, since trixie ships podman
# 5.4.2 and MIN_PODMAN was 5.7.0. That was the whole argument for measuring the floor: #94 is
# about package managers, and the largest excluded population was being excluded by a number
# instead. The floor is 4.9.0 now, so this machine is SUPPORTED, and the case asserts that the
# survey accepts it and the install proceeds.
#
# A SECOND RUNG ON THE VERSION LADDER, and that is the other reason it comes first.
# Containerfile.podman-old is ubuntu:24.04 at 4.9.3, which is two majors back and could
# plausibly be missing anything. 5.4.2 is three MINORS back, which is much harder to wave away,
# so it is the version the floor has to justify itself against. Whether it can is measured
# separately; see the MIN_PODMAN ladder in 26-installer-sandbox.sh.
#
# TIER A -- NO NESTING ADAPTATIONS, deliberately, and it is Containerfile.machine's five
# departures that are absent rather than anything added here:
#
#   * uid 1000, not 2. The uid-2 dance exists only so an inner subuid range can cover gid 65534
#     (Containerfile.machine:69-82); nothing here runs a nested podman, so the student is the
#     uid a student actually has.
#   * a real 200000-65536 subuid range, which is what the installer itself would write
#     (installer:653) rather than the 3:65534 an outer userns window forces.
#   * no vfs override, no cgroupfs override, no XDG_RUNTIME_DIR, no local repository.
#
# So this machine is MORE faithful than the one the rest of the tier runs on, and it is cheaper.
# machine_flags hands this base no capabilities and no devices at all.
#
# PINNED BY DIGEST, like Containerfile.machine, so `podman --version` inside cannot drift under
# the assertions that name 5.4.2. Measured before pinning: apt-cache policy in this image reports
# candidate 5.4.2+ds1-2+b2.
# PINNED BY MANIFEST-LIST DIGEST, not a per-architecture one -- the same rule
# 00-release-gates.sh:143 states and asserts for the course image. An earlier pin here named
# the amd64 LEG of this index, which meant the fixture ran emulated on Apple Silicon; the
# emulated amd64 podman then SIGSEGVd inside the Go runtime's netpoll and the survey read a
# register dump where it expected a version. One digest, both legs, native on each.
FROM debian:13@sha256:f324c7ff54321e8d9c588493a20244965938ce0aa50bbd1022d38010e9ffc4b1

# podman is the point; sudo, curl and openssh-client are here so that nothing ELSE needs doing and
# the refusal is the only thing the run can be about -- the same reasoning, and the same package
# line, as Containerfile.podman-old.
#
# uidmap IS ON THE LINE, and it has to be now. While MIN_PODMAN was 5.7.0 this machine was refused
# in the survey before the setuid helpers were ever probed, so leaving them out cost nothing. At
# MIN_PODMAN_LINUX=4.9.0 Debian's 5.4.2 is ACCEPTED, the survey carries on to the uidmap check
# (installer:431), finds them missing and asks to install them -- and this fixture has no local
# repository and runs with --network=none, so that install could not succeed. The case would have
# been about a missing package instead of about Debian being supported.
#
# --no-install-recommends otherwise, which is what keeps this fixture at a third of the machine's
# size: it leaves out buildah, criu, catatonit and dbus-user-session, none of which the survey
# looks for.
RUN apt-get update \
 && apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends sudo openssh-client curl podman uidmap \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

RUN if id -u 1000 >/dev/null 2>&1; then userdel -r "$(id -un 1000)" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true; fi \
 && useradd -m -u 1000 -s /bin/bash student \
 && printf 'student ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD:ALL\n' > /etc/sudoers.d/student \
 && chmod 0440 /etc/sudoers.d/student

# A range, so the subuid consent item cannot appear and confuse a refusal that should be the only
# thing in the transcript. Unreachable in practice for the reason above -- survey dies first --
# but a fixture whose state depends on the order of two checks is a fixture waiting to surprise
# somebody.
RUN printf 'student:200000:65536\n' > /etc/subuid \
 && printf 'student:200000:65536\n' > /etc/subgid

# The graph root's parent, pre-created student-owned. The single `podman --version` survey runs
# does not create it, but any podman command that touches the runtime does, and an ADDED path
# there reads as a change the installer made when it did not. Same treatment as podman-old.
RUN mkdir -p /home/student/.local/share/containers && chown -R student:student /home/student/.local

# The two tmpfs mount points the harness adds, so mounting over them is not itself a change.
RUN mkdir -p /var/tmp/shim /var/tmp/report && chmod 1777 /var/tmp/shim /var/tmp/report

# NO /etc/cs193v-fixture-id, unlike Containerfile.machine, and it is not an omission. That stamp
# exists so a nest probe can prove it ran inside the fixture rather than on the host; every
# assertion here reads the installer's transcript, which only the container produces, and
# `podman version 5.4.2` is itself proof of the boundary -- the host runs 5.7.0 or newer.
USER student
WORKDIR /home/student
